Architecture plan for Issue #85: Raise Server Actions body-size limit so post image uploads succeed

Project shape
- Next.js App Router application with TypeScript, React, Prisma/PostgreSQL, Tailwind, and S3-compatible object storage for blog images.
- Admin post editing is implemented with server actions in app/admin/posts/actions.ts and form UI components under app/admin/posts/_components/.
- Runtime configuration is centralized in lib/env/server.ts; object storage upload behavior is encapsulated in lib/storage/object-storage.ts.

Module/file organization
- next.config.ts: framework-level configuration. Issue #85 should be implemented here by adding the Server Actions body-size limit.
- app/admin/posts/actions.ts: existing createPost/updatePost server actions consume multipart FormData, extract File objects, and pass file.arrayBuffer() to storage. No architectural change expected for this issue.
- lib/storage/object-storage.ts: existing storage wrapper validates content type/size, prefixes object keys, uses concrete ContentLength, and signs reads. No change expected unless tests reveal upload constraints beyond Next's Server Actions limit.
- tests/e2e: useful place for an upload-regression test if existing Playwright coverage can be extended without heavy fixture setup.

Dependencies and issue ordering
- Only one issue is in scope. Issue #85 has no dependency on future issues.
- The fix depends on the existing multipart admin post editor and server action upload path already being present.

Key design decisions
- Set Next.js serverActions.bodySizeLimit to "25mb" in next.config.ts. This leaves application-level image validation unchanged while preventing Next from rejecting legitimate multipart submissions before createPost()/updatePost() run.
- Keep the storage-layer 10 MB per-image limit in lib/storage/object-storage.ts; the framework limit is intentionally higher to account for cover image, author avatar, inline image, and multipart overhead in one post form.
- Verify with lint/tests/build where practical, and confirm no secrets or runtime env values are committed.
